IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `ucpr-time`

-- Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 2005 (NSW) -- offers of compromise, costs and interest, from the seat of the claimant (the
-- plaintiff).
--
-- SEAT: the person bringing a civil claim in a NSW court. Every exported question is one that person would ask: "Is my
-- offer of compromise valid?", "What is the earliest closing date I can give it?", "The defendant has not paid what it
-- offered: what can I do?", "What costs order follows the offer?", "From when are costs on the indemnity basis?", "Does
-- recovering a small amount in a higher court stop my costs?", "What interest runs on my judgment?".
--
-- Provisions: r 20.25-20.29 (offers of compromise), r 42.1 (costs follow the event, as the fallback), r 42.13-42.17 (costs
-- consequences of offers), r 42.20 (costs on dismissal or striking out), r 42.34, 42.35 (costs where the claim belonged in
-- a lower court), r 36.7 (interest), with r 1.11 (time) from ucpr-time.l4.
--
-- ANSWERS. As in ucpr-proceedings.l4: JUST TRUE; JUST FALSE where the rule's own words say no; NOTHING (with a
-- "NOTHING-kind" comment) where the rules do not answer. "Unless the court orders otherwise" is stated in the question's
-- description and in the answer, not decided.
--
-- Based on content from the New South Wales Legislation website (as collected by the Open Australian Legal Corpus on
-- 10 March 2025; unofficial, possibly out of date). CC BY 4.0. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Uniform Civil Procedure Rules 2005 -- offers, costs and interest`

§§ `The nouns`

-- Who made the offer of compromise (r 20.26(1): "any party may ... make an offer to any other party").
DECLARE `Offeror` IS ONE OF `I made the offer`, `the defendant made the offer`

-- The parties and act of the regulative rule for payment under an accepted offer (r 20.26(8), 20.28, 20.29).
DECLARE `Offer party` IS ONE OF `the paying defendant`
DECLARE `Offer act` IS ONE OF `pay the sum or do the act the accepted offer provides for`

-- What follows when the defendant is to pay under an accepted offer.
DECLARE `Payment under an accepted offer` IS ONE OF
    `paid or done in time`
    `not yet due`
    `not paid in time: I may withdraw my acceptance, or elect judgment on the offer's terms or to have the defence struck out`
    `not paid in time: I may elect judgment on the offer's terms or to have the defence struck out`

-- The costs order the offer rules give (r 42.13A-42.17), unless the court orders otherwise.
DECLARE `Costs after an offer of compromise` IS ONE OF
    `the defendant pays my costs on the ordinary basis up to the time the offer was made`
    `I pay the defendant's costs on the ordinary basis up to the time the offer was made`
    `the accepted offer's own terms about costs apply`
    `the defendant pays my costs, on the ordinary basis and then on the indemnity basis`
    `the defendant pays my costs on the ordinary basis up to the switch, and I pay its costs on the indemnity basis after it`
    `I pay the defendant's costs, on the ordinary basis and then on the indemnity basis`
    `the offer rules give no costs order, so the general rule applies: costs follow the event unless the court orders otherwise`
    `not worked out here: the offer and the judgment are not both for a sum of money`

-- When costs switch to the indemnity basis (r 42.14(2)(b), 42.15(2)(b), 42.15A(2)(b)).
DECLARE `Starting point` IS ONE OF
    `from the beginning of` HAS `the switch day` IS A DATE
    `from 11 am on` HAS `the 11 am day` IS A DATE

-- What happened to the proceedings (r 42.20).
DECLARE `Dismissal or striking out` IS ONE OF
    `my proceedings, or part of them, were dismissed`
    `the defence, or part of it, was struck out`

-- Who pays the costs (r 42.20).
DECLARE `Costs on dismissal or striking out` IS ONE OF
    `I pay the defendant's costs, to the extent the proceedings were dismissed`
    `the defendant pays my costs of the matters on which the defence was struck out`
    `the court's order governs`

§§ `r 20.26 -- making an offer of compromise`

-- r 20.26(5) "The closing date for acceptance of an offer-- (a) in the case of an offer made two months or more before the
-- date set down for commencement of the trial--is to be no less than 28 days after the date on which the offer is made,
-- and (b) in any other case--is to be such date as is reasonable in the circumstances." Where no trial date is set the
-- offer cannot be "two months or more before" it, so (b) applies (fork F12; finding 4). Under (b) the date is the court's
-- judgment: NOTHING.
GIVEN `a trial date is set` IS A BOOLEAN, `the trial date` IS A DATE, `the day of the offer` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the offer is made two months or more before the trial` `a trial date is set` `the trial date` `the day of the offer` IF
    `a trial date is set` AND `the day of the offer` `is on or before` (`the trial date` `minus months` 2)

@export What is the earliest closing date I can give my offer of compromise (r 20.26(5))?
GIVEN `the day I make the offer` IS A DATE @desc The day you make (serve) the offer of compromise
      `a trial date has been set` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a date has been set down for the trial to begin
      `the day the trial begins` IS A DATE @desc The date set down for the start of the trial (any day if none is set)
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`What is the earliest closing date for my offer` MEANS
    IF `the offer is made two months or more before the trial` `a trial date has been set` `the day the trial begins` `the day I make the offer`
    THEN JUST (`the day I make the offer` `plus days` 28)
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (a judgment left to the court: "such date as is reasonable in the circumstances", r 20.26(5)(b))

-- r 20.26(2) "An offer under this rule-- (a) must identify-- (i) the claim or part of the claim to which it relates, and
-- (ii) the proposed orders for disposal of the claim ..., including, if a monetary judgment is proposed, the amount ...,
-- (b) if the offer relates only to part of a claim ..., must include a statement ... as to whether the balance of the
-- proceedings is to be abandoned or pursued [or defended or conceded], (c) must not include an amount for costs and must
-- not be expressed to be inclusive of costs, (d) must bear a statement to the effect that the offer is made in accordance
-- with these rules, (e) if the offeror has made or been ordered to make an interim payment to the offeree, must state
-- whether or not the offer is in addition to that interim payment, and (f) must specify the period of time within which
-- the offer is open for acceptance." r 20.26(3)(a)(ii) lets a defendant's offer of judgment in its favour include a sum
-- for the plaintiff's costs "despite subrule (2)(c)". A requirement not met answers JUST FALSE: the offer is not one made
-- under r 20.26, so the costs rules of Part 42 Division 3 do not follow it (fork F11).
@export Does an offer of compromise in my proceedings meet the requirements of rule 20.26, so that the costs rules for offers follow it?
GIVEN `the offer identifies the claim and the proposed orders` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer identifies the claim (or part) it relates to and the orders proposed, including the amount of any money judgment
      `the offer is for part of a claim only` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer relates only to part of a claim in the proceedings
      `it says what happens to the rest of the proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For an offer about part of a claim: whether it states whether the rest is to be abandoned or pursued (or defended or conceded)
      `the offer includes costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer includes an amount for costs, or is said to be inclusive of costs
      `it is a defendant's offer of judgment in its favour with a sum for my costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer is a defendant's offer of judgment in its own favour with a term that it pays you a specified sum for your costs (allowed by r 20.26(3)(a)(ii))
      `the offer says it is made in accordance with the rules` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer bears a statement that it is made in accordance with the Uniform Civil Procedure Rules
      `there is an interim payment between us` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person making the offer has made, or been ordered to make, an interim payment to the other party
      `the offer says whether it is in addition to the interim payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If so: whether the offer states whether or not it is in addition to that interim payment
      `the offer specifies the period it is open for acceptance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer specifies the period of time within which it is open for acceptance
      `the day the offer is made` IS A DATE @desc The day the offer is made
      `the closing date of the offer` IS A DATE @desc The last day on which the offer can be accepted
      `a trial date has been set` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a date has been set down for the trial to begin
      `the day the trial begins` IS A DATE @desc The date set down for the start of the trial (any day if none is set)
      `the closing date is reasonable in the circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Where the offer is made less than two months before the trial (or no trial date is set): whether the closing date is reasonable in the circumstances (the court's judgment)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the offer meet rule 20.26` MEANS
    IF     `the offer identifies the claim and the proposed orders`
       AND ((NOT `the offer is for part of a claim only`) OR `it says what happens to the rest of the proceedings`)
       AND ((NOT `the offer includes costs`) OR `it is a defendant's offer of judgment in its favour with a sum for my costs`)
       AND `the offer says it is made in accordance with the rules`
       AND ((NOT `there is an interim payment between us`) OR `the offer says whether it is in addition to the interim payment`)
       AND `the offer specifies the period it is open for acceptance`
       AND `the closing date is long enough`
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHERE
        `the closing date is long enough` MEANS
            IF `the offer is made two months or more before the trial` `a trial date has been set` `the day the trial begins` `the day the offer is made`
            THEN `is on or before` (`the day the offer is made` `plus days` 28) `the closing date of the offer`
            ELSE `the closing date is reasonable in the circumstances`

-- r 20.26(4) "If the offeror makes an offer before the offeree has been given such particulars of the offeror's claim, and
-- copies or originals of such documents ..., as are necessary to enable the offeree to fully consider the offer, the
-- offeree may, within 14 days of receiving the offer, give notice to the offeror that-- (a) the offeree is unable to assess
-- the reasonableness of the offer because of the lack of particulars or documents, and (b) ... the offeree will seek an
-- order of the court under rule 42.14(2)." An "if" condition and a period fixed in the rule's own words: JUST FALSE
-- outside them. With r 1.11(2) and (4).
@export Can I give the defendant notice that I cannot assess its offer for lack of particulars or documents (r 20.26(4))?
GIVEN `the offer came before I had the particulars and documents I need to consider it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant made the offer before giving you the particulars of its case and the documents you need to consider the offer fully
      `the day I received the offer` IS A DATE @desc The day you received the offer
      `the day I give the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day you give (or will give) the notice
      `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays
      `other days the registry is closed` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Every other day around the end of the 14 days on which the registry is closed (public holidays, the Christmas closure)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I give notice that I cannot assess the offer` MEANS
    IF     `the offer came before I had the particulars and documents I need to consider it`
       AND `is on or before` `the day I give the notice` (`the last day of` 14 `days after` `the day I received the offer` `under` (`registry closures` `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` `other days the registry is closed`))
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE

§§ `r 20.26(8), 20.28, 20.29 -- payment under an accepted offer (if the defendant has not paid)`

-- r 20.26(8) "Unless the notice of offer otherwise provides, an offer providing for the payment of money, or the doing of
-- any other act, is taken to provide for the payment of that money, or the doing of that act, within 28 days after
-- acceptance of the offer." r 20.28(1) "A party who accepts an offer may withdraw the acceptance ... (a) if the offer
-- provides for payment of money, or the doing of any other act, and the sum is not paid to the offeree or into court, or
-- the act is not done, within 28 days after acceptance of the offer or within such other time as the offer provides".
-- r 20.29(2) "If the defendant, being a party to an accepted offer, fails to comply with the terms of the offer, the
-- plaintiff is entitled-- (a) to such judgment or order as is appropriate to give effect to the terms of the accepted
-- offer, or (b) to an order that the defence be struck out, and to judgment accordingly, as the plaintiff elects, unless
-- the court orders otherwise." Time 0 is the day of acceptance; r 1.11(2), (4) apply.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Offer party` `Offer act`
`r 20.26(8) -- the defendant must pay or act within the time after acceptance` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the paying defendant`
    MUST `pay the sum or do the act the accepted offer provides for`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    LEST BREACH BY `the paying defendant` BECAUSE "the sum was not paid, or the act not done, within the time after acceptance (r 20.26(8), 20.28(1)(a))"

@export The defendant must pay me (or do something) under an accepted offer of compromise: has it done so in time, and if not, what can I do (r 20.26(8), 20.28, 20.29)?
GIVEN `I accepted the defendant's offer` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you accepted the defendant's offer (rather than the defendant accepting yours)
      `the day the offer was accepted` IS A DATE @desc The day the notice of acceptance was served
      `the offer gives its own time for payment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer says when the money is to be paid (or the act done)
      `the number of days the offer gives` IS A NUMBER @desc If so: how many days after acceptance the offer allows
      `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays
      `other days the registry is closed` IS A LIST OF DATE @desc Every other day around the end of the period on which the registry is closed (public holidays, the Christmas closure)
      `the defendant has paid or done it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the defendant has paid the sum to you or into court (or done the act)
      `the day it paid or did it` IS A DATE @desc The day it did so (any day if it has not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date, or the day you are asking about
GIVETH A `Payment under an accepted offer`
`Has the defendant paid under the accepted offer` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`r 20.26(8) -- the defendant must pay or act within the time after acceptance` (`the days from` `the day the offer was accepted` `to` `the last day`)) 0 `the events`
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN `paid or done in time`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `is on or before` `today` `the last day`
        THEN `not yet due`
        ELSE IF `I accepted the defendant's offer`
             THEN `not paid in time: I may withdraw my acceptance, or elect judgment on the offer's terms or to have the defence struck out`
             ELSE `not paid in time: I may elect judgment on the offer's terms or to have the defence struck out`
    WHERE
        `the days for payment` MEANS IF `the offer gives its own time for payment` THEN `the number of days the offer gives` ELSE 28
        `the last day` MEANS `the last day of` `the days for payment` `days after` `the day the offer was accepted` `under` (`registry closures` `the registry is closed on Saturdays and Sundays` `other days the registry is closed`)
        `paid by today` MEANS `the defendant has paid or done it` AND `is on or before` `the day it paid or did it` `today`
        `the events` MEANS
            IF `paid by today`
            THEN LIST (WAITUNTIL (`the days from` `the day the offer was accepted` `to` `the day it paid or did it`)), (EVENT `the paying defendant` `pay the sum or do the act the accepted offer provides for` (`the days from` `the day the offer was accepted` `to` `the day it paid or did it`))
            ELSE LIST (WAITUNTIL (`the days from` `the day the offer was accepted` `to` `today`))

§§ `r 42.13-42.17 -- costs after an offer of compromise`

-- r 42.13A (offer accepted, no provision for costs): "(2) If the offer proposed a judgment in favour of the plaintiff ...,
-- the plaintiff is entitled to an order against the defendant for the plaintiff's costs ..., assessed on the ordinary basis
-- up to the time when the offer was made. (3) If the offer proposed a judgment in favour of the defendant ..., the
-- defendant is entitled to an order against the plaintiff for the defendant's costs ..., assessed on the ordinary basis up
-- to the time when the offer was made."
-- r 42.14 (the plaintiff's offer not accepted, and the plaintiff obtains a judgment "no less favourable to the plaintiff
-- than the terms of the offer"): the plaintiff's costs, ordinary basis, then indemnity basis.
-- r 42.15 (the defendant's offer not accepted, and the plaintiff obtains a judgment "no more favourable to the plaintiff
-- than the terms of the offer"): the plaintiff's costs on the ordinary basis up to the switch; the defendant's costs on the
-- indemnity basis after it.
-- r 42.15A (the defendant's offer not accepted, and the defendant obtains a judgment "no less favourable to the defendant
-- than the terms of the offer"): the defendant's costs, ordinary basis, then indemnity basis.
-- r 42.16(1) in deciding which rule applies "the court must disregard so much of the interest ... as relates to the period
-- after the day on which the offer was made."
-- r 42.17(3) if, on the offeree's request, "the court is not satisfied that the party making the offer was at all material
-- times willing and able to carry out the offer, then, unless the court orders otherwise-- (a) rules 42.14 and 42.15 do not
-- apply". It does not name r 42.15A (finding 5).
-- Each "This rule applies if": when it fails, the offer rules give no order and r 42.1 (costs follow the event) is the
-- fallback. Only money offers and money judgments are compared here; other terms answer the "not worked out" value
-- (NOTHING-kind).
@export After an offer of compromise in my proceedings, what costs order do the offer rules give, unless the court orders otherwise (r 42.13A-42.17)?
GIVEN `who made the offer` IS AN `Offeror` @desc Who made the offer of compromise. One of: "I made the offer", "the defendant made the offer"
      `the offer was accepted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer was accepted
      `the accepted offer provided for costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it was accepted: whether its terms made provision for costs
      `the offer proposed judgment in my favour` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it was accepted: whether the offer proposed a judgment in your favour (rather than in the defendant's favour, or dismissal of your claim)
      `I obtained judgment in my favour` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it was not accepted: whether you obtained a judgment or order in your favour on the claim (rather than judgment for the defendant or dismissal)
      `the offer and the judgment are both for a sum of money` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offer and the judgment are both simply for a sum of money (so they can be compared by amount)
      `the amount of the offer` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of the money judgment the offer proposed, in dollars (costs are never included in an offer)
      `the amount of my judgment` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of your judgment in dollars, including any interest (0 if you lost)
      `the interest in my judgment after the day of the offer` IS A NUMBER @desc How much of the interest (or damages in the nature of interest) in your judgment relates to the period after the day the offer was made, in dollars
      `the offeree asked to be shown the offeror was willing and able` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the party to whom the offer was made asked the offeror to satisfy the court that it was at all times willing and able to carry out the offer (r 42.17)
      `the court was satisfied the offeror was willing and able` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If that was asked: whether the court was satisfied the offeror was at all material times willing and able to carry out the offer
GIVETH A `Costs after an offer of compromise`
`What costs order follows the offer` MEANS
    IF `the offer was accepted`
    THEN IF `the accepted offer provided for costs`
         THEN `the accepted offer's own terms about costs apply`
         ELSE IF `the offer proposed judgment in my favour`
              THEN `the defendant pays my costs on the ordinary basis up to the time the offer was made`
              ELSE `I pay the defendant's costs on the ordinary basis up to the time the offer was made`
    ELSE IF `I obtained judgment in my favour` AND (NOT `the offer and the judgment are both for a sum of money`)
         THEN `not worked out here: the offer and the judgment are not both for a sum of money` -- NOTHING-kind (the encoding compares money terms only)
         ELSE CONSIDER `who made the offer`
              WHEN `I made the offer` THEN
                  IF `I obtained judgment in my favour` AND `the judgment counted` AT LEAST `the amount of the offer` AND `rules 42.14 and 42.15 survive r 42.17`
                  THEN `the defendant pays my costs, on the ordinary basis and then on the indemnity basis`
                  ELSE `the offer rules give no costs order, so the general rule applies: costs follow the event unless the court orders otherwise`
              WHEN `the defendant made the offer` THEN
                  IF (NOT `I obtained judgment in my favour`)
                  THEN `I pay the defendant's costs, on the ordinary basis and then on the indemnity basis`
                  ELSE IF `the judgment counted` AT MOST `the amount of the offer` AND `rules 42.14 and 42.15 survive r 42.17`
                       THEN `the defendant pays my costs on the ordinary basis up to the switch, and I pay its costs on the indemnity basis after it`
                       ELSE `the offer rules give no costs order, so the general rule applies: costs follow the event unless the court orders otherwise`
    WHERE
        `the judgment counted` MEANS `the amount of my judgment` MINUS `the interest in my judgment after the day of the offer`
        `rules 42.14 and 42.15 survive r 42.17` MEANS (NOT `the offeree asked to be shown the offeror was willing and able`) OR `the court was satisfied the offeror was willing and able`

-- r 42.14(2)(b), 42.15(2)(b), 42.15A(2)(b): "(i) if the offer was made before the first day of the trial, as from the
-- beginning of the day following the day on which the offer was made, and (ii) if the offer was made on or after the first
-- day of the trial, as from 11 am on the day following the day on which the offer was made." r 1.11(4) does not move this
-- day: it is not a day for doing a thing.
@export Where the offer rules give costs on the indemnity basis, from when do they run (r 42.14(2)(b), 42.15(2)(b), 42.15A(2)(b))?
GIVEN `the day the offer was made` IS A DATE @desc The day the offer of compromise was made
      `the first day of the trial` IS A DATE @desc The first day of the trial (or the date set down for it)
GIVETH A `Starting point`
`From when do indemnity costs run` MEANS
    IF `the day the offer was made` `is before` `the first day of the trial`
    THEN `from the beginning of` (`the day the offer was made` `plus days` 1)
    ELSE `from 11 am on` (`the day the offer was made` `plus days` 1)

§§ `r 42.20 -- costs when proceedings are dismissed or a defence struck out`

-- r 42.20 "(1) If the court makes an order for the dismissal of proceedings ..., then, unless the court orders otherwise, the
-- plaintiff must pay the defendant's costs of the proceedings to the extent to which they have been dismissed. (2) If the
-- court makes an order striking out a defence ..., then, unless the court orders otherwise, the defendant must pay the
-- plaintiff's costs of the proceedings in relation to those matters in respect of which the defence has been struck out."
@export If my proceedings are dismissed, or the defendant's defence is struck out, who pays the costs (r 42.20)?
GIVEN `what happened` IS A `Dismissal or striking out` @desc What the court ordered. One of: "my proceedings, or part of them, were dismissed", "the defence, or part of it, was struck out"
      `the court ordered otherwise about costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court made a different order about the costs
GIVETH A `Costs on dismissal or striking out`
`Who pays the costs on dismissal or striking out` MEANS
    IF `the court ordered otherwise about costs`
    THEN `the court's order governs`
    ELSE CONSIDER `what happened`
         WHEN `my proceedings, or part of them, were dismissed` THEN `I pay the defendant's costs, to the extent the proceedings were dismissed`
         WHEN `the defence, or part of it, was struck out` THEN `the defendant pays my costs of the matters on which the defence was struck out`

§§ `r 42.34, 42.35 -- costs where the claim could have been brought in a lower court`

-- r 42.34 "(1) This rule applies if-- (a) in proceedings in the Supreme Court, other than defamation proceedings, a
-- plaintiff has obtained a judgment against the defendant or, if more than one defendant, against all the defendants, in
-- an amount of less than $500,000, and (b) the plaintiff would, apart from this rule, be entitled to an order for costs
-- ... (2) If the proceedings could have been commenced in the District Court, an order for costs must not be made unless
-- the Supreme Court is satisfied that the commencement and continuation of the proceedings in the Supreme Court was
-- warranted." r 42.35 is the same for the District Court, below $40,000, where the proceedings could have been commenced
-- in the Local Court. The answer is whether the rule stops the costs order: a flat "must not ... unless".
@export I won less than $500,000 in the Supreme Court (or less than $40,000 in the District Court): does that stop me getting a costs order (r 42.34, 42.35)?
GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court` @desc The court your proceedings are in. One of: "the Supreme Court", "the District Court", "the Local Court", "the Land and Environment Court", "the Dust Diseases Tribunal"
      `they are defamation proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings are defamation proceedings
      `my judgment is against the defendant, or against all of them` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you obtained judgment against the defendant (or, if more than one, against all of them)
      `the amount of my judgment` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount of your judgment, in dollars
      `I would otherwise be entitled to costs` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether, apart from this rule, you would be entitled to an order for costs against the defendant or defendants
      `the proceedings could have been started in the lower court` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the proceedings could have been commenced in the District Court (for a Supreme Court case) or the Local Court (for a District Court case)
      `the court is satisfied bringing them in this court was warranted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the court is satisfied that commencing and continuing the proceedings in the higher court was warranted (the court's judgment)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Does the lower court costs rule stop my costs order` MEANS
    IF `the rule applies` AND `the proceedings could have been started in the lower court` AND (NOT `the court is satisfied bringing them in this court was warranted`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE
    WHERE
        `the threshold` MEANS IF `the court` EQUALS `the Supreme Court` THEN 500000 ELSE 40000
        `the court is covered` MEANS
            (`the court` EQUALS `the Supreme Court` AND (NOT `they are defamation proceedings`)) OR `the court` EQUALS `the District Court`
        `the rule applies` MEANS
                `the court is covered`
            AND `my judgment is against the defendant, or against all of them`
            AND `the amount of my judgment` LESS THAN `the threshold`
            AND `I would otherwise be entitled to costs`

§§ `r 36.7 -- interest`

-- r 36.7(1) "The prescribed rate at which interest is payable under section 101 of the Civil Procedure Act 2005 is-- (a) in
-- respect of the period from 1 January to 30 June in any year--the rate that is 6% above the cash rate last published by
-- the Reserve Bank of Australia before that period commenced, and (b) in respect of the period from 1 July to 31 December
-- in any year--the rate that is 6% above the cash rate last published by the Reserve Bank of Australia before that period
-- commenced." The cash rate is a fact: the claimant states it, in percent a year.
@export What is the interest rate on my judgment debt for a half-year, under section 101 of the Civil Procedure Act 2005 (r 36.7(1))?
GIVEN `the cash rate before the half-year began` IS A NUMBER @desc The Reserve Bank of Australia cash rate last published before the half-year (1 January to 30 June, or 1 July to 31 December) began, in percent a year (for example 4.35)
GIVETH A NUMBER
`What is the interest rate on my judgment` MEANS `the cash rate before the half-year began` PLUS 6

-- r 36.7(2) "The Local Court may not order the payment of interest up to judgment in any proceedings in which the amount
-- claimed is less than $1,000." A flat negative: JUST FALSE below $1,000. Otherwise interest up to judgment is under
-- s 100 of the Civil Procedure Act 2005, outside these rules: NOTHING.
@export Can the court order the defendant to pay me interest up to judgment, as far as rule 36.7(2) is concerned?
GIVEN `the court` IS A `Court` @desc The court your proceedings are in. One of: "the Supreme Court", "the District Court", "the Local Court", "the Land and Environment Court", "the Dust Diseases Tribunal"
      `the amount I claim` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount you claim, in dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the court order interest up to judgment` MEANS
    IF `the court` EQUALS `the Local Court` AND `the amount I claim` LESS THAN 1000
    THEN JUST FALSE
    ELSE NOTHING -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the sources: interest up to judgment is under s 100 of the Civil Procedure Act 2005)
